Scott's Definition 2.1 is a structure \((D,\Delta,\mathrm{Con},\vdash)\) in which \(D\) is a set of data objects, \(\Delta\in D\) is least informative, \(\mathrm{Con}\) is a set of finite subsets of \(D\), and \(\vdash\) relates members of \(\mathrm{Con}\) to members of \(D\), subject to six axioms: a subset of a consistent set is consistent, every singleton is consistent, and inserting a token entailed by a consistent set preserves consistency; every consistent set entails \(\Delta\); a consistent set entails each of its members; and if a consistent set entails every member of another consistent set that entails \(X\), then it entails \(X\). The surrounding prose reads the tokens as finite data, \(\Delta\) as no information and hence as \(\bot\), failure of consistency as incompatibility on one element, and entailment as dependence, and it writes \(\mathbf{A}=(D_{\mathbf{A}},\Delta_{\mathbf{A}},\mathrm{Con}_{\mathbf{A}},\vdash_{\mathbf{A}})\). `InfoSys` states the same six axioms on a token type with decidable equality: consistency is downward closed under inclusion, every singleton is consistent, inserting an entailed token preserves consistency, every consistent set entails the least token, a consistent set entails each of its members, and if a consistent set entails every member of another consistent set then it entails whatever that second set entails. Decidable equality is the side condition used to form the finite-set operations; the six axioms are Scott's. The informal reading of \(\bot\) and of several systems at once adds no further axiom. **Theorem:** yes — the structure and the six axioms are `InfoSys`. **Proof:** yes — the note is the axiom list, and the gloss records those same six axioms.
